{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator, and leave the behaviour without the flag unchanged. Issue: https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5.\n\nWhat exists now:\n- bin/csv2json.js reads stdin with readFileSync(0, 'utf8'), calls parse(text) with no separator, ignores process.argv, and writes JSON.stringify(result) followed by \"\\n\".\n- src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','). It splits rows on /\\r?\\n/ and fields on sep.\n- test/csv.test.js uses node:test and already has a 'cli' test that runs execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }).\n- package.json: \"test\": \"node --test\".\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether '--tsv' appears anywhere in process.argv.slice(2). If it does, call parse(text, '\\t'). If it does not, call parse(text) exactly as now. Keep the output format unchanged: JSON.stringify of the result plus one \"\\n\". Do not add --sep/--delimiter, --help, separator auto-detection, quoting rules, or handling of unknown arguments. Leave src/parse.js unchanged unless you really need to change it, and keep its default ',' behaviour.\n2. In test/, add node:test tests that run bin/csv2json.js the way the existing 'cli' test does, passing args ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], and assert strict equality on stdout:\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' gives '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' gives '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' gives '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n'\n   Also add a test with no flag: input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' gives '[{\"a\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\t2\"}]\\n', where the output contains the JSON escape backslash-t. Keep the existing tests.\n3. In README.md, add a short section on the --tsv flag. Say that it reads tab-separated input, and include the example invocation `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. Keep the existing CSV example.\n4. From the repo root, check each of these:\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\" prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] and then exit=0\n   - printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv prints [{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\n   - printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js prints [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}], with a literal backslash-t in the output\n   - npm test exits 0\n5. Open one pull request that contains these changes and links the issue.","verifier":"Check the PR head from the repo root. The checks below follow the issue's expect lines one to one, in the same order. Use `| od -c` or `| cat -A` to confirm byte-exact output and the single trailing newline. Each check records PASS or FAIL with the command output.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: \"`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/o1; echo \"exit=$?\"; od -c /tmp/o1\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] plus one \\n, and exit=0. FAIL: any other output bytes, or a non-zero exit.\n\nCheck 2. Expect: \"With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c\nPASS: output is exactly [{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}] plus one \\n. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: \"With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c\nPASS: output is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}] plus one \\n. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 4. Expect: \"Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c\nPASS: output is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] plus one \\n. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 5. Expect: \"Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c\nPASS: output is exactly [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}] plus one \\n. Here \\t is the two-character JSON escape, a backslash followed by t, not a raw tab byte. FAIL: anything else, for example the keys split into a and b.\n\nCheck 6. Expect: \"`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\"\nRun: npm test; echo \"exit=$?\". Then run grep -n -- '--tsv' test/*.js and read the matching tests.\nPASS: exit=0, and at least one test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the --tsv argument on input that contains tab characters and asserts on its output. FAIL: npm test exits non-zero, or no such test exists, or the test only calls parse() directly, or it never checks the output.\n\nCheck 7. Expect: \"README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\"\nRun: grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md and read the surrounding text.\nPASS: README.md mentions the --tsv flag and includes an example invocation that uses it, such as node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv. FAIL: the flag is not mentioned, or there is no example invocation.\n\nAdvisory (not a PASS or FAIL condition):\n- Note whether --tsv also works in another argument position, for example with an extra argument before it.\n- Note any scope beyond the issue: a --sep option, --help, quoting rules, or a changed default in src/parse.js.\n- The existing test/csv.test.js tests should still be present."}